						<h1>The Ship That Started Coast2Road</h1>
						<p class="dek">When the <em>Juan Sebastián de Elcano</em> docked at our pier, an idea began to take shape.</p>
						<div class="meta"><span>From the archives</span><span>3 min read</span></div>
					</div>
				</header>

				<div class="article-layout">
					<aside>
						<p>Filed under</p>
						<span>Maritime</span>
						<span>Galveston</span>
						<span>Origin Story</span>
					</aside>

					<div class="article-body">
						<p class="lead">Coast2Road did not begin with a business plan or a carefully mapped-out content calendar. It began at a familiar pier on Pelican Island, when an extraordinary ship arrived at the Texas A&M Galveston campus.</p>

						<p>The Spanish Navy training ship <em>Juan Sebastián de Elcano</em> had docked where we worked. For maritime professionals, ships are part of everyday life—but this was not an everyday arrival. Her four masts, miles of rigging, bright white hull, and Spanish colors transformed the pier into something that felt connected to a much larger world.</p>

						<figure>
							<img src="/images/elcano-spanish-flag.jpg" alt="Spanish flag flying from the stern of Juan Sebastián de Elcano" />
							<figcaption>The Spanish flag and the name <em>Juan Sebastián de Elcano</em> at the stern.</figcaption>
						</figure>

						<h2>A working ship—and a floating classroom</h2>
						<p>The <em>Elcano</em> is the Spanish Navy’s training ship, carrying future naval officers to sea as part of their education. That mission made her visit especially fitting at the Texas A&M Galveston campus, where maritime work and education meet every day.</p>

						<p>There was ceremony in the visit, but also the unmistakable texture of a working vessel: lines under strain, sails secured to the yards, weathered steel, polished wood, officers on deck, and music carrying across the pier.</p>

						<figure>
							<img src="/images/elcano-crew.jpg" alt="Officers and musicians gathered on deck aboard Juan Sebastián de Elcano" />
							<figcaption>Crew and musicians gathered on deck during the Texas A&M Galveston campus visit.</figcaption>
						</figure>

						<blockquote>Sometimes the story comes to you. On this day, it tied up at our pier.</blockquote>

						<h2>The idea behind Coast2Road</h2>
						<p>Standing there with a camera, it was hard not to notice how many remarkable things pass through our working lives—and how easily those moments can disappear if no one pauses to document them.</p>

						<h2>The video that started it</h2>
						<p>This was the original video we posted from the visit. It reached roughly 6,000 views—far more than we expected—and showed us that other people were interested in the moments unfolding right outside our door. The sound is part of the story: the ship’s band was playing from the deck as we filmed.</p>

						<figure class="video-feature">
							<p class="sound-note"><span aria-hidden="true">♪</span> Sound on</p>
							<video controls playsinline preload="metadata" poster="/images/elcano-at-texas-am.jpg">
								<source src="/videos/elcano-original.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
								Your browser does not support embedded video.
							</video>
							<figcaption>The original Elcano video—the post that helped launch Coast2Road.</figcaption>
						</figure>

						<p>That impulse became Coast2Road: a place to share what we see through our work and in our own time, from ships and ports to boating, RV travel, food, museums, and the unexpected discoveries between destinations.</p>

						<p>The <em>Elcano</em> eventually sailed on. The idea stayed behind.</p>

						<footer class="article-end">
							<img src="/images/coast2road-compass.svg" alt="" />
							<p><span>From the coast to the road</span><strong>This is where our journey began.</strong></p>
